<article class="env-remark" id="09VS">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/09VS">Remark <span data-tag="09VS">14.19.11</span>.</a> We do not need all finite limits in order to be able to define the coskeleton functors. Here are some remarks </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
We have seen in Example <a href="/tag/0182" data-tag="0182">14.19.1</a> that if $\mathcal{C}$ has products of pairs of objects then $\text{cosk}_0$ exists. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
For $k > 0$ the functor $\text{cosk}_ k$ exists if $\mathcal{C}$ has finite connected limits. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> This is clear from the inductive procedure of constructing coskeleta (Remarks <a href="/tag/0189" data-tag="0189">14.19.8</a> and <a href="/tag/018A" data-tag="018A">14.19.9</a>) but it also follows from the fact that the categories $(\Delta /[n])_{\leq k}$ for $k \geq 1$ and $n \geq k + 1$ used in Lemma <a href="/tag/0183" data-tag="0183">14.19.2</a> are connected. Observe that we do not need the categories for $n \leq k$ by Lemma <a href="/tag/0184" data-tag="0184">14.19.3</a> or Lemma <a href="/tag/0185" data-tag="0185">14.19.4</a>. (As $k$ gets higher the categories $(\Delta /[n])_{\leq k}$ for $k \geq 1$ and $n \geq k + 1$ are more and more connected in a topological sense.) </p>
</article>